## 1. Scope
The scope of this test strategy is the authenticated avatar upload and crop capability associated with Issue ID AD-488. This strategy covers the complete ingestion journey beginning with browser-level access gating and extending through API Gateway routing, synchronous malware inspection, structural file header validation, storage commitment into the authorized cloud ecosystem, CDN edge cache invalidation, and real-time avatar propagation across active user views and shared header components. The feature is not treated as a simple file upload. It is a chained, stateful transaction that crosses UI, gateway, security validation, storage, cache, and rendering layers. As a result, the scope must be framed as an end-to-end service workflow with strict ordering guarantees and measurable release risk controls rather than as an isolated front-end component enhancement.

Within scope are the controls that determine whether an authenticated user with a valid, unexpired browser cookie session can see and interact with the avatar modification experience. This includes upload entry-point visibility, authorized interaction with the file drop area, single-image transaction behavior, crop-related request handling, enforcement of the 5MB file-size boundary, JPEG and WebP whitelist validation, rejection of spoofed or renamed files using magic-number inspection, and proof that server-side-only processing is preserved with no browser-side compression, scaling, or image manipulation outside the intended crop interaction. The strategy also includes orchestration verification proving that malware scanning is synchronous and inline, that no object is committed to storage prior to scan completion, and that no CDN invalidation or UI refresh occurs after a failed security verdict.

The scope also includes non-functional and integration behaviors that are essential to business acceptance. These include end-to-end timing validation against the 2.0-second SLA under a standard 4G network proxy restriction, gateway rejection of invalid or expired authentication contexts with 401 Unauthorized responses, edge purge verification across CDN propagation checkpoints, and dynamic DOM-based refresh validation ensuring avatar updates appear across all active views without a full page reload. Multi-session synchronization is explicitly in scope because the acceptance criteria require immediate consistency across user-visible surfaces. Production-like observability, timing checkpoints, audit-log correlation, and resilience evidence are therefore included as mandatory validation mechanisms.

### 1.1 In Scope
- Authenticated access control for avatar modification interface visibility and interaction
- Single avatar image upload and crop workflow for public profile display
- API Gateway routing, filtering, and rejection handling
- Server-side processing validation with raw client binary transfer
- Synchronous inline malware scanning before storage commitment
- File signature and magic-number validation for JPEG and WebP
- 5MB boundary enforcement including exact 5.00MB acceptance and 5.01MB rejection
- Authorized cloud bucket storage verification
- Immediate CDN cache invalidation after successful update
- Real-time avatar synchronization across active sessions, profile views, and shared header bars
- Dynamic DOM refresh verification without full browser reload
- Audit logging and event correlation for critical upload lifecycle steps
- SLA validation under standard 4G throttling conditions
- Fault handling for malware scan failures, gateway rejection, and storage-path interruptions

### 1.2 Assumptions
- Authentication token generation and refresh mechanisms are already implemented and can be simulated in test environments.
- Authorized cloud storage, CDN invalidation hooks, malware scan harnesses, and gateway observability are available in QA and staging.
- Crop behavior is executed by supported backend services or trusted service endpoints and can be validated via request and output assertions.
- Log aggregation and timing telemetry are accessible to the QA and release teams.

### 1.3 Dependencies
- API Gateway configuration and access logs
- Session/token fixtures for valid, expired, and missing authentication states
- Malware scanner harness or test double supporting clean and infected-file verdicts
- Authorized cloud bucket and folder assertions
- CDN purge event visibility and regional response sampling
- Browser instrumentation for page reload, canvas usage, and network-path inspection
- CI/CD pipelines for automation, reporting, and gated promotion

## 2. Test Items
The primary test item is the authenticated avatar upload and crop feature delivered for AD-488. Subcomponents include the front-end avatar control, upload drop zone, crop parameter submission path, API Gateway upload route, malware inspection service, file-type/header validation service, cloud storage persistence tier, CDN purge trigger, profile rendering services, and SPA refresh logic. Secondary test items include audit-log generation, security event reporting, and observability data used to prove sequencing, timing, and failure isolation.

Because the architecture is explicitly server-side for processing, every test item will be treated as part of a controlled transaction chain rather than a standalone technical component. The item set therefore includes not only positive-path upload behavior, but also bypass-prevention checks ensuring uploads do not route directly to bucket endpoints, protection against unauthorized access, and handling of malformed payloads, oversized binaries, and infected files.

## 3. Quality Objectives
The primary quality objective is trustable identity presentation through a secure, performant, and consistent avatar update experience. Business value depends on users being able to recognize accounts quickly, but that value is realized only if the upload path cannot be abused for malware ingress, spoofed content, unauthorized profile mutation, or stale edge-cache exposure. Therefore, quality is defined as a combination of correctness, security, timing predictability, and visual synchronization. This objective mandates validation beyond UI success messaging; the system must prove that only authenticated users can initiate the workflow, that invalid files never persist, and that all downstream systems reflect the change in a coordinated and observable manner.

A second quality objective is operational determinism across a multi-layer architecture. The acceptance criteria require exact boundary enforcement at 5MB, exact rejection behavior for unsupported or disguised file formats, and exact sequence control for malware scanning before storage. These controls are highly sensitive to integration drift between gateway policies, validation microservices, object storage rules, and CDN purge behavior. The strategy therefore prioritizes deterministic verification with logs, timing checkpoints, and traceability from requirement to executable tests. Quality is not achieved if the feature works intermittently; it is achieved when the same control outcomes occur repeatedly across environments, browsers, network conditions, and failure modes.

A third objective is release readiness under realistic delivery constraints. The avatar update must complete within 2.0 seconds under standard 4G throttling, which requires timing confidence under both single-user and expected concurrent conditions. The strategy must therefore confirm that baseline functionality, synchronization, and security checks remain intact while performance and resilience are measured. The release will be considered ready only when the capability demonstrates controlled defect containment, auditable execution evidence, stable automation coverage, and risk mitigations acceptable to engineering, product, and operations stakeholders.

### 3.1 Entry Criteria
- Approved scope boundaries and verified final test approach received from prior pipeline stages
- Test environments provisioned with gateway, scanner, storage, CDN, and observability connectivity
- Test data prepared for valid, boundary, invalid, spoofed, and infected files
- CI/CD pipeline configured for automated execution and result publication
- Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 manual validation granted before release-publish interpretation

### 3.2 Exit Criteria
- All critical and high-priority functional, security, and integration scenarios executed
- No open Sev-1 or Sev-2 defects related to auth bypass, malware bypass, invalid storage commit, cache stale content, or SLA failure
- Risk mitigation actions approved for any accepted medium-risk residual issues
- Requirement traceability demonstrates full coverage of in-scope objectives
- Audit and execution evidence retained in approved repositories

#### 3.2.1 In Scope
- All scope elements listed in Section 1.1 are subject to execution and reporting.

#### 3.2.2 Out of Scope
- Client-side compression tools are excluded because the architecture explicitly prohibits browser-side image processing beyond intended user interaction controls.
- Local image scaling engines and front-end canvas manipulation scripts are outside scope because the implementation requires server-side processing and validation.
- PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design file support are excluded because the whitelist is restricted to JPEG and WebP only.
- Concurrent batch uploads and multi-image profile header adjustments are excluded because the business workflow is a single-avatar transaction only.
- Storage infrastructures outside the authorized cloud ecosystem are excluded because they violate deployment boundaries and would not represent supported runtime behavior.

## 4. Test Approach
The test approach uses a layered verification model spanning Component/UI, API/Gateway, Service/Integration, System End-to-End, Production-like Non-Functional, and Security & Audit Log Verification levels. Execution begins with front-end authentication gating and controlled UI state validation, then progresses into gateway contract and boundary enforcement, followed by service orchestration checks proving sequencing of scan, storage, purge, and UI propagation. The strategy uses requirement-based traceability to connect each acceptance criterion and extracted objective to one or more automated or manually assisted test assets.

Design techniques include equivalence partitioning for token states, decision tables for visibility and access outcomes, boundary value analysis at the 5MB cap, error guessing for malformed or renamed payloads, state transition testing for upload lifecycle progression, cause-effect mapping across acceptance conditions, compatibility validation across supported browsers, and fault injection for gateway, scan, and storage failures. This model is intentionally evidence-heavy because several requirements, such as synchronous malware inspection and no full page reload, cannot be proven by UI outcomes alone. They require network tracing, log correlation, and instrumentation.

### 4.1 Test Types
This project requires broad test-type coverage because the feature combines user interaction, security validation, infrastructure dependencies, and latency-sensitive propagation. Testing will be executed across Dev where service contracts and early automation are stabilized, QA where integration behavior is validated comprehensively, UAT where business-visible suitability is confirmed, Pre-Prod where release confidence and operational rehearsal are established, and Prod where tightly controlled smoke verification confirms safe deployment. The timeline emphasizes earlier execution of component, contract, and negative-path suites, followed by staged non-functional and resilience verification once service dependencies are stable.

