Requirement traceability will be maintained dynamically through Jira-linked test cases and automation identifiers embedded in CI/CD execution reports. Each acceptance criterion and extracted objective receives a unique mapping key that is referenced in automated test names, result dashboards, and defect records. This enables release stakeholders to determine not merely whether tests passed, but exactly which scope boundary or non-functional obligation is covered by each asset.

Operationally, every pull request that changes avatar upload, auth middleware, gateway policy, storage integration, malware scanning integration, or SPA rendering logic must include an updated traceability impact declaration. Test case references, automation tags, and impacted requirements will be reviewed during peer review. Nightly pipeline reports will aggregate coverage at the requirement level so that regressions can be identified against business expectations rather than hidden in aggregate pass percentages. This is essential for AD-488 because several high-risk controls, such as auth gating and file-header validation, may appear stable in summary metrics while masking critical requirement-specific drift.

### 4.3 Test Management
Test management for AD-488 will follow a controlled lifecycle spanning planning, design, implementation, execution, defect triage, re-test, regression, and release recommendation. Jira will act as the system of record for requirement references, test execution cycles, defects, and risk acceptances. Test assets will be partitioned into smoke, component/UI, gateway/API, integration, synchronization, security, and performance suites so that the CI/CD pipeline can execute the right level of coverage at the right stage. A mandatory branch policy will require pull-request linkage to the relevant Jira story or defect, evidence of impacted test assets, and peer-review sign-off before merge. No code affecting upload, auth, validation, storage, CDN, or rendering paths should be merged without automated checks passing and test-impact notes being attached.

Execution management will be aligned to the pipeline. On every pull request, Playwright smoke checks and PyTest API contract suites will validate core gating, upload route behavior, and obvious regression risk. On merge to the integration branch, expanded suites will run including boundary conditions, spoofed-file rejection, crop validations, gateway route proof, and storage/CDN integration checks. Nightly jobs will run multi-session synchronization, audit-log correlation, and broader negative-path coverage. Pre-Prod execution will introduce JMeter-driven SLA and concurrency patterns under tcconfig-enforced 4G conditions, while OWASP ZAP scans will inspect the exposed upload surface for misconfiguration and response weaknesses. Human review gates will remain active at key promotion points so results are interpreted in business and operational context rather than auto-promoted blindly.

Defect triage will occur daily during active execution windows, with severity determined by business risk and exploitability rather than by UI symptom alone. For example, any defect allowing unauthorized view or interaction with avatar controls, allowing bypass of malware scan or magic-number validation, storing invalid files, or failing to invalidate CDN caches after successful upload will be treated as Sev-1 or Sev-2 depending on reproducibility and blast radius. Each defect must contain reproducible steps, requirement references, environment details, logs, screenshots or HAR files where relevant, and evidence of expected versus actual sequence behavior. Re-test and regression are mandatory after fixes, and defect closure requires linked test evidence in Jira.

#### 4.3.1 Test Organization
QA ownership resides with the Senior QA Director and designated automation engineers for browser, API, and performance workstreams. Development teams own unit-level safeguards, feature flags, and remediation support. DevOps supports environment readiness, observability, and pipeline integration. Security provides guidance and sign-off for malware, spoofed file, and gateway exposure findings.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
Test materials will be stored in a version-controlled repository aligned with the application release branch strategy, with automation separated into browser, API/integration, performance, and security folders for maintainability. Playwright scripts, PyTest suites, JMeter plans, ZAP baseline configurations, and tcconfig profiles will be versioned alongside supporting fixtures and data builders. Repository ownership will be shared between QA automation leads and engineering maintainers, with branch protections ensuring that any change to tests receives peer review and that deletion of critical coverage requires explicit approval.

Tracking updates will be maintained through pull-request templates that require issue linkage, test-scope declarations, and execution evidence. CI-generated reports will publish artifact links back into Jira and the repository commit history so changes in coverage can be audited over time. This repository discipline is particularly important for AD-488 because subtle logic changes in auth middleware, gateway routing, or cache invalidation triggers can silently invalidate existing automation assumptions if not reviewed carefully.

#### 4.3.3 Recording Pass/Fail Results
A test will be recorded as Pass only when all expected assertions for that scenario succeed, including both user-visible outcomes and hidden integration or telemetry checks where those checks are part of the acceptance proof. For example, a valid upload scenario is not considered Pass simply because an avatar is displayed. It must also demonstrate correct gateway routing, completed malware inspection, storage in the approved bucket path, successful purge evidence, and synchronized UI propagation without a full reload if those are listed assertions within the scenario. A test is Fail if any mandatory assertion does not hold, if environment instability blocks reliable interpretation, or if evidence capture is incomplete for a high-risk control.

Automated results will be captured directly from CI/CD jobs and linked into Jira test execution records. Manual or exploratory observations used for targeted release qualification must be recorded with structured evidence templates. Flaky or inconclusive results will not be marked Pass; they will be categorized separately and investigated through quarantine or rerun policies. This prevents false confidence, especially in timing-sensitive and event-driven workflows such as CDN propagation or multi-session avatar refresh.

### 4.4 Defect Management
Defect management will use Jira workflows configured to reflect the actual release-control needs of this feature: Open, Triaged, In Progress, Ready for QA, Retest, Verified, Deferred, and Closed. Every defect must be mapped to one or more requirement IDs from the traceability matrix and classified by severity, priority, component, environment, and release impact. Peer review is required before defect closure when a fix changes security validation, gateway rules, or synchronization logic. This is necessary because a fix that resolves one symptom may unintentionally weaken another control in the upload chain.

Defects discovered in CI will be auto-filed or enriched through pipeline integrations containing build number, branch, failing suite, stack traces, and links to logs or artifacts. Defects found during manual release assessment must include comparable evidence, including browser recordings, network traces, API payloads, log extracts, and timing data where applicable. A daily triage forum involving QA, engineering, product, and DevOps will decide remediation windows, rollback criteria, and whether residual risk is acceptable for release. No medium-or-higher defect may be silently accepted without documented rationale and stakeholder approval.

### 4.5 Test Data
Test data will include clean and infected JPEG/WebP samples, exact-size 5.00MB files, over-limit 5.01MB samples, malformed binaries, renamed text and script payloads, varied crop coordinate combinations, expired and missing authentication cookies, and multiple user-session combinations for synchronization validation. Data will be curated so each scenario has deterministic expected outcomes and can be safely reused across environments.

