### 4.6 Test Environments
The core QA integration environment must mirror the browser UI, API Gateway, validation and malware scan service, authorized cloud bucket path, profile service, CDN invalidation route, and SPA rendering layer. This environment must support session injection, gateway log access, controlled scanner outcomes, bucket inspection, and purge-event visibility. Without these hooks, multiple critical requirements cannot be proven and the environment should not be treated as release-representative.

A production-like staging or Pre-Prod environment is required for SLA and resilience validation. It must allow tcconfig-based network shaping to emulate standard 4G conditions, JMeter-driven concurrent upload flows, and regionally sampled CDN checks. Compatibility coverage should include supported Chromium-based desktop browsers, Firefox, and Safari-equivalent validation where platform support requires it. Multiple active browser sessions must be supported to prove immediate avatar synchronization across profile and header surfaces.

### 4.7 Test Automation Strategy
Automation is central to this feature because the risk profile spans deterministic validations that are too broad and too repeatable to rely on manual execution. Playwright will automate browser-level validation of auth gating, upload interaction, crop flow behaviors, dynamic DOM refresh, and multi-session UI consistency. Its strengths in browser context control, network interception, and event observation make it appropriate for proving no full page reload occurs and for correlating visible changes with backend-triggered state updates. Playwright suites will run as pull-request smoke tests for high-value flows and as broader nightly regressions for synchronization and compatibility scenarios.

PyTest will serve as the primary framework for API, integration, storage, and evidence-centric validations. It will validate gateway response contracts, file-size and magic-number boundaries, synchronous malware scan sequencing, correct bucket placement, and CDN purge triggers. PyTest is also well suited for combining API calls, fixture management, backend assertions, and log correlation in a maintainable pattern. JMeter will be introduced for SLA and concurrency validation, especially to measure whether the complete upload-to-distribution flow remains within the 2.0-second target under representative user load and 4G throttling. tcconfig will be used in the execution environment to enforce repeatable network conditions. OWASP ZAP will complement these frameworks by probing the upload attack surface for misconfiguration, insecure responses, and unexpected exposure paths.

Automation will be integrated into CI/CD in tiers. Pull requests must pass Playwright smoke and PyTest contract suites before merge. Integration-branch builds will run extended negative, crop, security, storage, and purge validations. Nightly pipelines will execute full regression packs plus audit-log verification. Pre-Prod pipelines will trigger JMeter performance plans and selected ZAP checks, with human review required before promotion. Peer review is mandatory for automation changes, with reviewers verifying requirement tags, assertion quality, fixture hygiene, and result stability. Flaky tests will be quarantined only with explicit issue linkage, remediation owners, and expiry dates; they cannot be silently muted because this feature’s controls are too critical for untracked instability.

### 4.8 Test Results and Reporting
Execution reporting will include pass/fail status, defect linkage, requirement coverage, environment, build identifier, and evidence artifacts. Dashboards should show both suite health and requirement-level health, since aggregate suite pass percentages can obscure failures in critical security or synchronization controls. Release reporting must distinguish environment failures from product failures and explicitly flag any residual risk.

#### 4.8.1 Reporting Cadence
- Pull Request: automated smoke and contract results posted per build
- Daily: defect triage summary and execution health report
- Nightly: regression coverage dashboard with trend comparison
- Pre-Prod: release qualification report including SLA and risk review
- Release Gate: formal recommendation with residual-risk disposition

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
Historical test execution logs, build artifacts, performance reports, ZAP outputs, browser traces, screenshots, and gateway/log-correlation evidence will be stored in the CI artifact repository and linked back to Jira and source control commits. Retention should be sufficient to support release audits, defect root-cause analysis, and post-release incident review, with a minimum retention window aligned to enterprise policy and not less than one full release cycle.

Test result maintenance will include version tagging by build and branch, requirement-coverage snapshots, and archival of performance baselines so drift can be detected over time. For AD-488, historical evidence is especially valuable because the most serious risks involve regressions in timing, sequencing, and synchronization that may not be apparent from a single build’s result set.

### 4.9 Tools
Tool / Framework | Purpose | Integration Strategy | Owner
---|---|---|---
Playwright | Browser automation for auth gating, upload UI, crop flow, SPA refresh, and multi-session synchronization validation | Executes in PR, nightly, and release pipelines; captures traces, videos, DOM events, and browser-network evidence; linked to Jira and CI artifacts | QA Automation Team
PyTest | API, gateway, storage, integration, and log-correlation validation | Runs contract, boundary, security, and orchestration checks in CI; uses fixtures for auth states, files, bucket assertions, and scanner outcomes | QA Automation Team
JMeter | Performance and concurrency testing for 2.0-second SLA validation | Invoked in QA and Pre-Prod performance stages; produces timing distributions and throughput data under shaped network conditions | Performance Engineering
OWASP ZAP | Security baseline and attack-surface analysis for upload endpoints and gateway exposure | Scheduled baseline scans and targeted validation in integration and Pre-Prod; findings linked to Jira security workflow | Security QA
tcconfig | Network shaping to emulate standard 4G conditions for repeatable SLA validation | Applied to performance runners and staging nodes to enforce upload/download/latency constraints before JMeter and timed test execution | DevOps / Performance Engineering

These tools were selected because they map directly to the architecture and risk profile rather than satisfying generic automation preferences. Playwright is the best fit for proving hidden/disabled UI states, multi-session synchronization, and no-full-reload behavior. PyTest covers backend-rich validations where fine-grained assertions, fixtures, and logs are essential. JMeter and tcconfig together produce credible non-functional evidence instead of optimistic local timings. OWASP ZAP provides a practical additional security layer for the upload surface and gateway configuration.

