## 6. Test Schedule / Milestone
A high-level execution estimate for AD-488 is as follows: planning and traceability setup will require approximately 2 business days; automation design and data preparation will require 3 to 4 business days; Playwright and PyTest implementation for core functional, negative, crop, and integration scenarios will require 5 to 7 business days; environment hardening, scanner harness alignment, and CDN observability setup will require 3 business days in parallel; JMeter and tcconfig performance preparation will require 2 to 3 business days; Pre-Prod execution, defect triage, and release recommendation will require 3 to 4 business days depending on defect volume. This yields an estimated end-to-end effort window of roughly 3 working weeks with overlapping activities, assuming stable environment availability.

Human resources are expected to include 1 QA lead, 2 automation engineers, 1 performance engineer part-time, 1 security QA resource part-time, 1 DevOps engineer part-time, and 2 developers for defect turnaround and instrumentation support. If CDN sampling, malware harness behavior, or gateway observability are delayed, schedule variance of 2 to 4 business days should be expected. The timeline should therefore be managed with explicit dependency checkpoints rather than fixed-date optimism.

## 7. Suspension and Resumption Criteria
Execution will be suspended if critical environments are unavailable, malware harness outputs are unreliable, gateway logs cannot be accessed, CDN purge evidence cannot be collected, or token-state simulation is invalid. Execution will resume when root cause is corrected, impacted suites are re-baselined if necessary, and QA leadership approves continuation. Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 requires a paused state pending manual validation before final release interpretation is accepted.

## 8. Test Deliverables
- Approved enterprise test strategy
- Requirement traceability matrix
- Automated Playwright, PyTest, JMeter, and ZAP assets
- Test data packs and fixture definitions
- CI/CD execution reports and retained artifacts
- Defect reports with severity and traceability mapping
- Performance baseline and SLA assessment
- Release risk summary and go/no-go recommendation

## 9. Risk Mitigation Table
Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation Actions | Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Dynamic gateway-to-malware-scan connection drops could produce ambiguous outcomes where the upload request appears accepted by the client while the scan verdict is delayed or lost, creating uncertainty about whether storage or purge actions were attempted. | Medium | High | High | 1) Implement transaction IDs propagated across gateway, scanner, storage, and purge logs. 2) Add PyTest integration checks proving the request remains blocked until a scan verdict is returned. 3) Validate no partial object write occurs during scan interruption. 4) Add retry and explicit failure contract assertions for scanner connectivity faults. 5) Block release if interrupted scans can result in indeterminate client messaging or orphaned objects. | QA + Engineering
R2 | SLA processing latencies under standard 4G conditions may exceed 2.0 seconds when malware scan time, object storage write time, and CDN invalidation occur together, especially under moderate concurrent usage. | Medium | High | High | 1) Use tcconfig and JMeter to establish repeatable 4G latency profiles and concurrency baselines. 2) Capture timing checkpoints for upload, scan, storage, purge, and UI refresh. 3) Tune gateway timeout and payload handling thresholds. 4) Optimize scanner and purge invocation paths if bottlenecks exceed budget. 5) Require Pre-Prod evidence at target concurrency before release approval. | Performance Engineering
R3 | Mock service desynchronization or non-production scanner doubles may not faithfully represent synchronous inline malware behavior, leading to false confidence that no storage occurs before scan completion. | Medium | High | High | 1) Maintain contract tests between the real scanner interface and test doubles. 2) Run targeted validation against production-like scanner behavior in Pre-Prod. 3) Correlate timestamps across gateway and storage logs to prove ordering. 4) Add negative-path cases for infected files and scanner failures. 5) Reject release evidence derived solely from happy-path mocks. | QA + Security QA
R4 | Authentication token expiration timing and browser cookie drift may create inconsistent gating results where UI visibility, gateway authorization, and backend session interpretation disagree across active sessions. | High | High | High | 1) Build decision-table coverage for valid, missing, and expired tokens. 2) Simulate token aging and replay scenarios in Playwright and PyTest. 3) Correlate UI state with gateway 401 logs. 4) Validate active-session synchronization behavior when one session expires mid-flow. 5) Add regression checks to PR pipelines for all auth states. | QA Automation Team
R5 | CDN purge desynchronization or regional edge-cache lag may cause some users to see stale avatars after a successful upload, creating a business-visible inconsistency despite a technically successful transaction. | Medium | High | High | 1) Capture purge event IDs and correlate them with upload transaction IDs. 2) Sample multiple edge responses after upload and inspect cache headers. 3) Validate multi-session rendering across profile and header components. 4) Define rollback or temporary cache-busting guidance if lag exceeds tolerance. 5) Require regional evidence in Pre-Prod before sign-off. | DevOps / SRE
R6 | Direct-to-bucket bypass or misconfigured gateway routing could allow unsupported upload paths that evade centralized filtering, size enforcement, logging, or malware inspection. | Low | Very High | High | 1) Execute bypass attempts against bucket and alternate endpoints using PyTest and OWASP ZAP. 2) Verify only gateway-issued routes accept traffic. 3) Review access policies and log denied attempts. 4) Enforce infrastructure-as-code reviews for route and bucket policy changes. 5) Treat any confirmed bypass as release-blocking. | Security QA + DevOps

